    explanation: `BJJ, and by extension MMA's ground game, treats positions as sitting on a rough hierarchy from least to most dominant: guard (roughly even, though variations differ), half guard (slightly favouring top), side control (clearly favouring top), mount (strongly favouring top), and back control (generally regarded as the single most dominant position in the sport, for either gender or the fighter using it). A fighter's tactical priority on the ground is usually to advance up this hierarchy when on top, and to prevent that advance, or escape back down it, when underneath.`,
    howItWorks: `The hierarchy reflects how much control a position gives the top fighter and how limited the bottom fighter's offensive and defensive options become as position worsens for them. From guard, the bottom fighter still has both legs actively involved in control and attack; from mount or back control, the bottom fighter's usable tools shrink dramatically, and simply surviving and escaping becomes the realistic priority rather than attacking. This is why commentary treats a fighter conceding back control very differently from a fighter conceding guard, even though both are "on the ground and not on top."`,
    example: `A fighter passes an opponent's guard to side control, then works from there to mount, and finally takes the back as the opponent scrambles to escape mount. Each step up that sequence represents a real, judged improvement in position, even though the fight has not been finished and no single moment looked as dramatic as a strike landing.`,
    whyItMatters: `Understanding the hierarchy is what lets a viewer read a ground exchange's stakes correctly: a fighter conceding mount after controlling top position for most of a round has just given up a great deal, not merely shuffled positions, and a fighter escaping back control to guard has achieved a significant defensive win even without reversing the position outright.`,
