    howItWorks: `A fighter working a cage takedown typically first establishes control against the fence, often via an underhook or a body lock, before changing levels or off-balancing the opponent to bring them down, sometimes finishing the takedown by running the opponent's hips along the fence rather than attempting a single clean lift or trip. The fence gives the attacking fighter a stable reference point to drive against that open space simply does not offer.`,
    example: `A fighter unable to complete an open-space double-leg attempt against a sprawling opponent instead works to back that opponent against the cage first, then uses a body lock and the fence itself for leverage to bring the fight to the ground, succeeding where the identical technique failed moments earlier in open space.`,
    whyItMatters: `Recognising cage takedowns as a distinct, generally higher-percentage category from open-space takedowns explains why fighters with a wrestling background so often work to establish cage position before committing fully to a takedown attempt.`,
