    explanation: `**Cage control** describes a fighter using the fence to pin an opponent in place, limiting that opponent's movement and options while dictating the pace and location of the exchange. It is judged as a scoring factor in MMA (specifically as "control of the fighting area"), separate from whether any strikes or a takedown are actually landed during the control.`,
    howItWorks: `A fighter working cage control typically maintains upper-body control, an underhook, a collar tie, or a body lock, while using their own base and the fence to prevent the opponent from creating space or escaping laterally along the fence. Cage control can be used to set up a takedown, to land short strikes in the clinch, or simply to control the pace and location of a round without necessarily advancing to any further action.`,
    example: `A fighter presses an opponent against the cage for a significant stretch of a round, controlling with an underhook and preventing any meaningful counter-offence, without ever completing a takedown or landing significant strikes. Depending on how actively that control is being used, this can still be credited by judges as meaningful control of the fighting area.`,
    whyItMatters: `Cage control being a distinct, judged criterion, separate from strikes landed or takedowns completed, is one of the more debated aspects of MMA scoring, and understanding it is necessary to follow why a fight that looked one-sided in cage time can still be scored closely if strikes and other factors are weighed more heavily.`,
    misunderstandings: `A common one: assuming cage control alone automatically wins a round regardless of what else happens. It is one factor among several judges weigh, and its value can be discounted if the control is not seen as sufficiently active; see "Why Cage Control Alone May Not Win a Round" for the full picture.`,
