    explanation: `**Cage escapes** describes the range of methods a fighter uses to break out of an opponent's control against the fence and return the fight to open space or a neutral clinch position, rather than remaining pinned and controlled. This is distinct from a takedown defence or a specific escape technique; it is the broader positional goal those individual tools serve.`,
    howItWorks: `Escaping the cage typically involves first disrupting the controlling fighter's grip or head position, an underhook fight, a duck-under, or simply creating enough separation with a push or a level change, then circling off the fence rather than moving straight back into it. Circling, moving laterally along or away from the fence rather than retreating straight back, is generally favoured because moving straight back tends to run a fighter right back into the fence they were just trying to leave.`,
