    howItWorks: `A fighter controlling an opponent against the cage, typically via an underhook or collar tie, uses the free hand or a knee to land strikes at close range, while the controlling grip prevents the opponent from creating the distance needed to strike back effectively or escape. The strikes and the control tend to work together: landing strikes can create small openings to improve grip position, and improved grip position in turn makes further strikes easier to land.`,
    example: `A fighter with a collar tie against the cage lands a series of short knees to the opponent's midsection, using the tie to control posture and prevent the opponent from stepping back to create striking distance of their own, accumulating damage across an extended clinch exchange rather than through any single decisive strike.`,
    whyItMatters: `Cage clinch striking is a significant, if visually less dramatic, source of both damage and scoring activity in many fights, and recognising it as a deliberate tactic rather than incidental scuffling explains why some fighters specifically work to establish and hold cage clinch position rather than disengaging to strike at range.`,
