    theTechnique: `**Catching** a kick means trapping an opponent's kicking leg with the arms as it arrives, rather than blocking or avoiding it, which leaves the kicking fighter momentarily balanced on one leg and vulnerable to a follow-up. A caught kick can be used to set up an immediate takedown, a leg sweep of the opponent's remaining standing leg, or simply to disrupt their balance and rhythm.`,
    whenUsed: `Catching is generally attempted against kicks thrown with enough commitment and a clear enough trajectory to be trapped, most often mid-level kicks, and is a higher-risk, higher-reward option compared with checking or simply blocking, since a missed catch attempt can leave a fighter with no defence against the kick at all.`,
    risks: `Attempting to catch a kick and failing leaves a fighter with essentially no defence against that strike, having committed to trapping it rather than blocking or avoiding it; a fighter can also be caught off balance themselves if the kicking fighter anticipates the catch and immediately follows with a strike or a takedown of their own.`,
    counters: `An opponent aware that kicks are being caught can vary kick height, commit less fully to kicks so they can be retracted quickly, or immediately follow a caught kick with a strike or a takedown attempt of their own before the catching fighter can capitalise on the position.`,
