    explanation: `**Defensive footwork** covers the specific stepping patterns and positioning a fighter uses to avoid being squarely in front of an opponent's strikes or takedown attempts, working alongside distance management as one of the sport's least visually dramatic but most consistently valuable defensive tools. Rather than reacting to a specific strike once it is thrown, defensive footwork is largely about denying an opponent a clean, favourable angle to attack from in the first place.`,
    howItWorks: `Good defensive footwork typically involves small, controlled steps, often at an angle rather than straight backward, to move off the centreline an opponent is squared up to attack along, while maintaining enough balance to counter or defend whatever comes next. Moving straight backward is generally considered less effective than angling off, since a fighter retreating in a straight line can be tracked down and cornered, particularly against the cage, more easily than one who steps off at an angle.`,
    example: `Rather than backing straight away from pressure, a fighter steps at a slight angle each time an opponent advances, gradually repositioning so the opponent has to reset and re-square their stance repeatedly rather than simply walking the fighter down in a straight line toward the cage.`,
    whyItMatters: `Footwork that denies an opponent a clean angle is often what separates a fighter who looks difficult to hit or take down from one who is simply better at absorbing or blocking strikes once they arrive; the former is generally the more sustainable defensive approach over a full fight.`,
    misunderstandings: `A common one: treating all backward movement as equally good defensive footwork. Retreating in a straight line is considerably easier for an opponent to track and cut off, especially near the cage, than angling laterally, even though both look superficially similar as "moving away."`,
    takeaways: `Defensive footwork denies an opponent a clean angle to attack from, and angling off rather than retreating straight back is generally the more effective version of it, particularly with the cage limiting how far a straight retreat can go.`,
