    explanation: `A **scramble** is a fast, often chaotic exchange in which position is briefly unsettled and up for grabs, most commonly around a takedown attempt, a sweep, or a failed pass. Used defensively, a fighter in a bad position can deliberately create or capitalise on a scramble as an escape route, since the resulting confusion can offer a path out of a position that would be very difficult to escape through a slower, more methodical approach.`,
    howItWorks: `A fighter working to scramble out of a bad position typically looks to disrupt the opponent's control just enough to create a brief window where neither fighter has settled position, then moves decisively during that window rather than waiting for it to resolve into another clear position. Because a scramble is inherently unstable, both fighters are working to be the one who lands in the advantageous spot once it settles, so a defensive scramble is a genuine gamble, but often a better one than remaining in a clearly bad, settled position.`,
    example: `A fighter pinned in a bad position without an obvious clean escape available deliberately explodes into movement during an opponent's transition attempt, creating a brief scramble in which neither fighter has full control. Rather than ending up worse off, the fighter uses the resulting chaos to scramble to a neutral or even improved position, an outcome that a slower, more careful escape attempt from the settled position might not have allowed.`,
    whyItMatters: `Recognising scrambling as a deliberate defensive option, rather than simply "things getting messy," explains why some fighters known for scrambling ability can escape positions that look, on paper, nearly impossible to get out of through more conventional, methodical escapes.`,
